            <!-- Start Neuroticism: -->
            <section>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="vertical-space">
                        Neuroticism
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                        Neuroticism refers to the extent to which
                        individuals experience negative emotions such
                        as anxiety, depression, irritability, and moodiness.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        Individuals who score <span class="bold-text">high
                            in neuroticism</span> exhibit several
                        characteristic traits and
                        tendencies that distinguish them from those who
                        score lower on this personality
                        dimension.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Here are some key aspects of people high in
                        neuroticism:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Emotional
                                Sensitivity:</span> High neuroticism
                            individuals tend to
                            be highly sensitive to
                            their own emotions and the emotions of others.
                            They may experience intense
                            feelings of anxiety, worry, sadness, and fear in
                            response to stressors or perceived
                            threats.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Negative
                                Emotionality:</span> Neurotic individuals
                            often experience
                            a heightened frequency
                            and intensity of negative emotions compared to
                            those low in neuroticism. They may
                            be more prone to feelings of insecurity,
                            self-doubt, guilt, and shame, which can
                            impact their overall well-being and quality of
                            life.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Tendency towards
                                Stress:</span> People high in neuroticism
                            may be
                            more susceptible to
                            stress and may have difficulty coping with
                            stressful situations or life events. They may
                            ruminate over past mistakes or worry excessively
                            about future uncertainties, leading
                            to feelings of overwhelm and distress.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Mood Swings:</span>
                            Neurotic individuals may experience frequent and
                            unpredictable shifts
                            in mood and affect. They may go from feeling
                            elated and optimistic one moment to
                            feeling despondent and pessimistic the next,
                            making their emotional state volatile
                            and unpredictable.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Catastrophizing:</span>
                            High neuroticism individuals may have a
                            tendency to catastrophize,
                            or magnify the significance of negative events
                            or outcomes. They may perceive minor
                            setbacks or inconveniences as major disasters,
                            leading to heightened levels of anxiety
                            and distress.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Hypersensitivity to
                                Criticism:</span> Neurotic individuals may
                            be
                            highly sensitive to criticism
                            or perceived slights from others. They may take
                            negative feedback personally and
                            may dwell on perceived shortcomings or failures,
                            leading to feelings of inadequacy
                            and self-criticism.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Health Anxiety:</span>
                            People high in neuroticism may be more prone
                            to health-related
                            anxiety and hypochondria. They may worry
                            excessively about their health and wellbeing,
                            interpreting minor physical sensations or
                            symptoms as signs of serious illness
                            or disease.
                        </li>
                        <li class="list-item">
                            <span class="bold-text">Difficulty in
                                Relaxation:</span> Neurotic individuals may
                            have
                            difficulty relaxing and
                            unwinding, even in situations where relaxation
                            is warranted. They may struggle to
                            quiet their racing thoughts and may find it
                            challenging to experience moments of
