                    </h3>
                    <p>
                        An interest area refers to a specific aspect or
                        domain of activities, topics, or
                        subjects that individuals find appealing, enjoyable,
                        or engaging. It reflects their
                        preferences, inclinations, and passions in various
                        areas of life, such as hobbies,
                        leisure activities, or career pursuits. Interest
                        areas can encompass a wide range of
                        interests, from artistic and creative pursuits to
                        scientific exploration, social
                        interaction, or hands-on activities. Identifying
                        one's interest areas is essential for
                        understanding personal preferences, strengths, and
                        motivations, and it can help
                        individuals make informed decisions about their
                        career paths, educational
                        pursuits, and leisure activities. By exploring and
                        nurturing their interests,
                        individuals can find fulfillment, satisfaction, and
                        purpose in their lives.
                    </p>
                    <p class="vertical-space">
                        The six interest areas:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <span class="bold-text">Building</span> (Realistic):
                        Building individuals are practical,
                        hands-on, and enjoy working
                        with tools, machinery, and physical objects. They
                        are often mechanically inclined
                        and prefer tasks that involve physical activity and
                        problem-solving. Careers that
                        align with the Building interest area include
                        carpentry, engineering, farming,
                        construction, and skilled trades.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        <span class="bold-text">Thinking</span>
                        (Investigative): Thinking individuals are
                        analytical,
                        curious, and enjoy
                        exploring ideas and solving complex problems. They
                        are drawn to careers that
                        involve research, analysis, and experimentation.
                        Careers that align with the
                        Thinking interest area include science, engineering,
                        computer programming,
                        research, and academia.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        <span class="bold-text">Creating</span> (Artistic):
                        Creating individuals are creative,
                        imaginative, and enjoy
                        expressing themselves through art, music, writing,
                        or design. They are drawn to
                        careers that allow them to use their creativity and
                        originality to produce unique
                        works. Careers that align with the Creating interest
                        area include graphic design,
                        writing, photography, music, theater, and interior
                        design.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        <span class="bold-text">Helping</span> (Social):
                        Helping individuals are empathetic, outgoing,
                        and enjoy working
                        with people to help and support them. They are drawn
                        to careers that involve
                        helping others, such as teaching, counseling,
                        healthcare, social work, and
                        community organizing. They thrive in roles that
                        allow them to make a positive
                        impact on others' lives.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        <span class="bold-text">Persuading</span>
                        (Enterprising): Persuading individuals are
                        ambitious,
                        persuasive, and
                        enjoy taking on leadership roles and pursuing
                        opportunities for success. They are
                        drawn to careers that involve sales, marketing,
                        entrepreneurship, and business
                        management. They excel in roles that allow them to
                        influence others and achieve
                        their goals.
                    </p>
                    <p class="top-extra-space">
                        <span class="bold-text">Organizing</span>
                        (Conventional): Organizing individuals are
                        organized,
                        detail-oriented,
                        and enjoy working with data, numbers, and systems.
                        They are drawn to careers
                        that involve following established procedures,
                        managing information, and
                        maintaining order. Careers that align with the
                        Organizing interest area include
                        accounting, administrative roles, banking, data
                        analysis, and office management.
                    </p>
